<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>Section 4.3: Inverses of Trig Functions</title>

  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>

  <p>
    Sine, cosine, and tangent all fail the horizontal line test unless we 
    restrict the angle.  So we <em>only</em> have an inverse trig function
    if the angle is chosen very carefully. See below:
  </p>

    <image width="35%" margins="0% 65%">
      <shortdescription>
        The graph of sine.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-3-0a">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="5">
          <definition>f(x)=sin(x)</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi-1,-1.4,3*pi/2+1,1.4)">
            <grid-axes ylabel="y=sin(x)" h-pi-format="yes" spacings="((-pi,pi/2,3*pi/2),(-1,1,1))" hlabels="(-pi,pi/2,3*pi/2)"/>
            <graph function="f" />
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>


  <image width="35%" margins="0% 65%">
      <shortdescription>
        The graph of cosine.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-3-0b">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="5">
          <definition>f(x)=cos(x)</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi-1,-1.4,3*pi/2+1,1.4)">
            <grid-axes ylabel="y=cos(x)" h-pi-format="yes" spacings="((-pi,pi/2,3*pi/2),(-1,1,1))" hlabels="(-pi,pi/2,3*pi/2)"/>
            <graph function="f" />
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

    <image width="35%" margins="0% 65%">
      <shortdescription>
        The graph of sine.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-3-0c">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="5">
          <definition>f(x)=tan(x)</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi-1,-4.4,3*pi/2+1,4.4)">
            <grid-axes  h-pi-format="yes" spacings="((-pi,pi/2,3*pi/2),(-1,1,1))" hlabels="(-pi,pi/2,3*pi/2)"/>
            <graph function="f" />
            <label anchor="(3,4)"><m>y=\tan(x)</m></label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

  

  <activity>
  <title>Arccosine</title>
  
  
    <introduction>
      
      <p>Consider the <m>g(t)</m> to be only the part of 
      <m>\cos(t)</m> function restricted to 
      <m>[0,\pi]</m>:</p>
      
      
    </introduction>

    
    <task workspace="0cm">
    
      <statement>
        <p>What is the domain of <m>g</m>? (Not <m>\cos(t)</m>!) What is the 
        range of <m>g</m>?  Does <m>g</m> pass the horizontal line test? Does <m>g</m> have 
        an inverse function?</p> 

        <image width="45%" margins="0% 55%">
      <shortdescription>
        The graph of cosine and g(t) which is cosine restricted between [0,pi].
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-3-0d">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="18">
          <definition>f(x)=cos(x)</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi-1,-1.4,3*pi/2+1,1.5)">
            <grid-axes xlabel="t" ylabel="g(t)" h-pi-format="yes" spacings="((-pi,pi/2,3*pi/2),(-1,1,1))" hlabels="(-pi,pi/2,3*pi/2)"/>
            <graph function="f" dash="4 4" />
            <graph function="f" domain="(0,pi)" thickness="4" stroke="red"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace=".8cm">
      <statement>
        <p>We know <m>\cos(\frac{\pi}{4}) = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>, so since <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m> is in <m>[0,\pi]</m>, then <m>g(\frac{\pi}{4}) = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>.  What then is <m>g^{-1}(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2})</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2cm">
      <statement>
        <p>Use your unit circle to find the following:  
        <ul cols="3">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>g^{-1}(-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}) </m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>g^{-1}(-\frac12)</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>g^{-1}(0) </m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>


  

  <definition>
    <title>Definition 4.3.2</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>g(t)=\cos(t)</m> defined ONLY on the domain <m>[0,\pi]</m>, and note the range is <m>[-1,1]</m>.  For any real number <m>y</m> where <m>-1\le y \le 1</m>, the <term>arccosine of <m>y</m></term> is denoted <md>t=\arccos(y)</md> and is the angle <m>t</m> where <m>0\le t \le \pi</m> and <m>\cos(t)=y</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

<sidebyside widths="50% 40%">
  

  <image >
      <shortdescription>
        The graph of restricted cosine on [0,pi] and arccosine.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-3-0e">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="18">
          <definition>f(x)=cos(x)</definition>
          <definition>g(x)=acos(x)</definition>
          <definition>h(x)=x</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi-1,-1.4,3*pi/2+1,3.4)">
            <grid-axes xlabel="t"  h-pi-format="yes" spacings="((-pi,pi/2,3*pi/2),(-1,1,1))" hlabels="(-pi,pi/2,3*pi/2)"/>
            <graph function="f" domain="(0,pi)" thickness="2" stroke="red"/>
            <graph function="g" domain="(-1,1)" stroke="blue" thickness="4"/>
            <graph function="h" dash="4 4" stroke="black"/>
            <label anchor="(-2.4,2.2)"><m>y=arccos(t)</m></label>
            <label anchor="(3.9,-1.4)"><m>y=cos(t)</m></label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>



  <p workspace=".15in"><term>** What does arccosine output???  An ANGLE! **</term></p>
</sidebyside>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 4.3.2</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Use the special points on the unit circle to determine the exact values of each of the following numerical expressions. Do so without using a computational device.</p>

        <p>
          <ol cols="3" marker="a.">
            <li>
              <p><m>\arccos(\frac12)</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>\arccos(\frac{\sqrt{2}}2)</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>\arccos(\frac{\sqrt{3}}2)</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>\arccos(-\frac12)</m></p>
            </li>
  
            <li>
              <p><m>\arccos(-\frac{\sqrt{3}}2)</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>\arccos(-1)</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>\arccos(0)</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>\cos(\arccos(-\frac12))</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>\arccos(\cos(\frac{7\pi}6))</m></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
   </activity>

  <p><term>Arcsine</term></p>

  <definition workspace="0in">
    <title>Definition 4.3.3</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>f(t)=\sin(t)</m> defined <em>only</em> on the 
      domain <m>[-\frac{\pi}2,\frac{\pi}2]</m>, and note the 
      range is <m>[-1,1]</m>.  For any real number <m>y</m> 
      where <m>-1\le y \le 1</m>, the <term>arcsine of <m>y</m></term> 
      is denoted <md>t=\arcsin(y)</md> and is the angle <m>t</m> 
      where <m>-\frac{\pi}2 \le t \le \frac{\pi}2</m> and 
      <m>\sin(t)=y</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 4.3.3</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>The goal of this activity is to understand key properties of the arcsine function in a way similar to our discussion of the arccosine function.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>According to the definition of arcsine above, what is the domain of arcsine?  The range?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use your unit circle and your knowledge of inverses to determine exactly:  (Recall, all answers are angles between <m>-\frac{\pi}{2}</m> and <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>).
        <ul cols="5">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\arcsin(-1)</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\arcsin(-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2})</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\arcsin(0)</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\arcsin(\frac12)</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\arcsin(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2})</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>Sketch a careful plot of the restricted sine function on the interval <m>-\frac{\pi}2 \le t \le \frac{\pi}2</m>.  Then plot the inverse, <m>\arcsin(t)</m> on the same graph.  
 (They should look like reflections of each other across the line <m>y=x</m>, do they?)</p>
<image width="35%" margins="0% 65%">
      <shortdescription>
        The graph of restricted cosine on [0,pi] and arccosine.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-3-3">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(250,200)" margins="15">
          <definition>f(x)=sin(x)</definition>

          <coordinates bbox="(-2,-2,2,2)">
            <grid-axes xlabel="t"  ylabel="y"  />
            <graph function="f" domain="(-pi/2,pi/2)" thickness="2" stroke="red"/>
            <graph function="g" domain="(-1,1)" stroke="blue" thickness="4"/>
            <label anchor="(1.8,.8)"><m>sin(t)</m></label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
 
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace=".5cm">
      <statement>
        <p>True or false:  <m>\arcsin(\sin(5\pi)) = 5\pi</m>.  Why/why not?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <p><term>Arctangent</term></p>
  <p>** Note: <m>\tan(t)</m> does not exist everywhere!  So we need to choose where to restrict it to very carefully...</p>

  <definition workspace="0.15in">
    <title>Definition 4.3.5</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>f(t)=\tan(t)</m> defined ONLY on the domain <m>(-\frac{\pi}2,\frac{\pi}2)</m>, and note the range is all real numbers!  For any real number <m>y</m>, the <term>arctangent of <m>y</m></term> is denoted <md>t=\arctan(y)</md> and is the angle <m>t</m> where <m>-\frac{\pi}2 &lt; t &lt; \frac{\pi}2</m> and <m>\tan(t)=y</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 4.3.4</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>The goal of this activity is to understand key properties of the arctangent function.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace=".3in">
      <statement>
        <p>According to the definition of arctangent above, what is the domain of arctangent?  The range?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace=".4in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use your unit circle and your knowledge of inverses to determine exactly:  (Recall, all answers are angles between <m>-\frac{\pi}{2}</m> and <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>).
        <ul cols="4">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\arctan(-\sqrt{3})</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\arctan(-1)</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\arctan(0)</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\arctan(\frac1{\sqrt{3}})</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1cm">
      <statement>
        <p>The restricted tangent function on the interval <m>-\frac{\pi}2 &lt; t &lt; \frac{\pi}2</m> is shown.  Plot the inverse, <m>\arctan(t)</m> on the same graph.</p>
        <p>(Note, a vertical asymptote on the original function corresponds to what kind of asymptote on the inverse function?)</p>
        <image width="35%" margins="0% 65%">
      <shortdescription>
        The graph of restricted tangent on (-pi/2,pi/2).
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-3-4">
      
        <diagram dimensions="(250,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>f(x)=tan(x)</definition>

          <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
            <grid-axes xlabel="t"  ylabel="y"  />
            <graph function="f" domain="(-pi/2,pi/2)" thickness="2" stroke="red"/>
            <label anchor="(2.3,3.4)"><m>tan(t)</m></label>
            <line dash="2 2" endpoints="((-pi/2,-5),(-pi/2,5))"/>
            <line dash="2 2" endpoints="((pi/2,-5),(pi/2,5))"/>
            <label anchor="(2.3,3.4)"><m>tan(t)</m></label>
            <label anchor="(-2.5,-3.4)"><m>t=-\frac{\pi}{2}</m></label>
            <label anchor="(2.5,-3.4)"><m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m></label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Complete the sentence: <q>as <m>t</m> increases without bound (as <m>t\rightarrow \infty</m>), the function <m>\arctan(t)</m>...</q></p>

  
      </statement>
    </task>
    
  </activity>

  <activity>
  <title>Practice</title>
  
  
    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>Solve the equations below exactly. Give your answers in radians, and find <em>all</em> possible values for <m>t</m>
 in the interval <m>0\le t\le 2\pi</m>.
 There may be more than one answer!</p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>\sin(t)=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>, when <m>t=</m><fillin/></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\cos(t)=\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m>, when <m>t=</m><fillin/></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\tan(t)=-\sqrt3</m>, when <m>t=</m><fillin/></p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose <m>\sin(t)=\frac{x}{5}</m> and <m>t</m> is in the first quadrant.  Draw a right triangle that matches this criteria.  Can you find an expression for <m>\cos(t)</m>?  For <m>\tan(t)</m>?  Both expressions will involve <m>x</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

</handout>
